  thing elsewhere in the window, or BeOS's way of doing it;
- anything else that looks unfinished or wrong.
Only look: run no commands, read no file but this brief and these
pictures, start no agent or subagent of any kind. For each problem say
which picture, where in it, what's wrong, and what it should be. If you
find nothing, say so plainly; don't invent.

How to look. You have nothing that measures, so don't try to work out
exact pixel positions by reasoning about them: judge by eye, as a
careful person would with the picture in front of them, and say
"about". Go through each picture once, closely, then write: a few
minutes for the whole review is right. Don't go round the same picture
again to prove or disprove what you first thought; what you aren't sure
of, write as a thing for the lead to check ("check whether the well's
right edge meets the button's"), not as a finding, and go on.

Write the whole review, findings only, the most important first, as
Markdown to {out} with your file tool (that one file, nothing else),
then reply DONE and wait: the lead may ask more.

The pictures:
{pictures}
"""


